  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="d21eb73e-0532-407c-8c17-041efa2bbf99" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+7\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-3\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-3)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=x^2-3\). The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-3\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-3)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="6e27480e-e352-49be-9b6f-44f63467d9a5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+7\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,0)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=x^2\). The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,0)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bfabbcb1-2b5d-4d2e-8a10-460822d6bb93" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+7\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+7\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,7)\) and the coefficient of the term \(f(x)=x^2+7\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=x^2+7\). The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+7\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,7)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ba4aa0c4-e1a5-4e68-874d-f6737f811d7f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2-3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+7\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(f(x)=x^2+5\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=x^2+5\). The graph represents \(f(x)=x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="94861d4e-d17e-4df1-9d09-0e8774e8e2c6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>factored form \(f(x)=(x+2)(x+3)\) 
        standard form \(f(x)=x^2+5x+6\)</para>
            <para>The following two equations are equivalent.</para>
            <para> Which equation helps find the \(x\)-intercepts most efficiently?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The equation written in factored form \(f(x)=(x+2)(x+3)\) because the factors in the expression allow us to see when the expression is 0.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is factored form. The equation written in factored form \(f(x)=(x+2)(x+3) \) because the factors in the expression allow us to see when the expression is 0. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e021a697-88ee-4d81-a205-df68f64b7376" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which equation helps find the \(y\)-intercept most efficiently?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The equation written in standard form \(f(x)=x^2+5x+6\) because the constant term shows us the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is standard form. The equation written in standard form \(f(x)=x^2+5x+6\) because the constant term shows us the \(y\)-intercept. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="549853a5-000e-484f-90c1-79f2bae27211" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2\) (black graph, at the top, opening upward)
        \(f(x)=x^2-4\)
        \(f(x)=-x^2+5\)</para>
            <para> There are three equations represented on this graph:</para>
            <para>Use the graph to answer questions a and b.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_13_01.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para> Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x^2-4\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The blue graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-4\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-4)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2. </para>
            <para> The correct answer is blue graph (in the middle, opening upward). The blue graph represents \(f(x)=x^2-4\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-4)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="10205e3d-e739-41b1-8b9e-175685ffe51b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=-x^2+5\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The green graph represents \(f(x)=-x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is green graph (at the bottom, opening downward). The green graph represents \(f(x)=-x^2+5\) because the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,5)\) and the coefficient of the term \(x^2\) is a negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="4c5dd1eb-ea08-432e-bf96-40ef5667ac83" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Select two equations whose graphs have a positive \(y\)-intercept.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(f(x)=(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(x+1)(x+2)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are: \(f(x)=(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(x+1)(x+2)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="5b8cb81c-00b1-4688-b76b-3c1b4ab07a79" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which function represents the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) shifted 5 units to the right?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2+5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^2-5\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=(x+5)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=(x-5)^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The function is \(f(x)=(x-5)^2\) because subtracting 5 from \(x\) shifts the graph to the right 5 units.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=(x-5)^2\). Subtracting 5 from \(x\) shifts the graph to the right 5 units. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="0abd8816-8058-492b-ba67-a32d84901a2c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> equations whose graphs open downward.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(f(x)=1-x^2\), \(f(x)=-(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(1-x)(x+2)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2 and activity 7.12.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are: \(f(x)=1-x^2\), \(f(x)=-(x-1)^2\), \(f(x)=(1-x)(x+2)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="40de6698-d5bb-4a0c-9372-f40f8f6b0a44" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which curve is the narrowest?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=3x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=(3x)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=\frac13x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(\frac13x)}^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The function is \(f(x)=(3x)^2\) is the narrowest because multiplying x by a value greater than 1 makes the curve grow faster. \((3x)^2 =9x^2\) , so \(f(x)=(3x)^2\) is the narrowest curve.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=(3x)^2\). Multiplying \(x^2\) by a value greater than 1 makes the curve narrower. \((3x)^2 =9x^2\) , so \(f(x)=(3x)^2\) is the narrowest curve. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="59bc177a-332c-4bc1-b521-4f21086e378d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x-4)}^2+4\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x+4)}^2+4\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=-{(x+4)}^2+4\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\) because the graph is moved 4 units right and 4 units up and the squared term is multiplied by negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\). The graph represents \(f(x)=-{(x-4)}^2+4\) because the graph is moved 4 units right and 4 units up and the squared term is multiplied by negative 1, so the graph should open downward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="46ffb136-295e-46ce-9789-3497e2b34928" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x-2)}^2+3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x-3)}^2+2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x+2)}^2+3\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x)={(x+3)}^2+2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph represents \(f(x)=(x+2)^2+3\) because the graph is moved 2 units left and 3 units up and the squared term is multiplied by positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(f(x)=(x+2)^2+3\). The graph represents \(f(x)=(x+2)^2+3\) because the graph is moved 2 units left and 3 units up and the squared term is multiplied by positive 1, so the graph should open upward. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1ce92a00-0789-432e-9eee-cadf08545987" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 13 - 14, use the following graph.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_13_02.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Which of the graphs has been horizontally stretched? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Graph b</para>
            <para>Graph a</para>
            <para>Graph c</para>
            <para>Graph d</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Graph d</para>
            <para>Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph d</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="0a7c4d1b-b296-460c-b1de-e2e27b90f8d1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which function most closely represents the equation for graph c?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x - 2.5)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (4x)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (x + 2.5)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(f(x) = (\frac14x)^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! <emphasis/></para>
            <para>Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is <emphasis/></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
